$$\begin{align*}
\tan\left(\theta+\frac{\pi}{6}\right) &= \frac{\tan\theta+\tan\frac{\pi}{6}}{1-\tan\theta\tan\frac{\pi}{6}} \\
&= \frac{\tan\theta+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}}{1-\frac{\tan\theta}{\sqrt{3}}} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{3}\tan\theta+1}{\sqrt{3}-\tan\theta} \\
&= \frac{1+\sqrt{3}\tan\theta}{\sqrt{3}-\tan\theta}
\end{align*}$$

Since $\tan\theta=\sqrt{3}$ does not satisfy the original equation, divide both sides by $\sqrt{3}-\tan\theta$:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{1+\sqrt{3}\tan\theta}{\sqrt{3}-\tan\theta} &= \tan(\pi-\theta) \\
\tan\left(\theta+\frac{\pi}{6}\right) &= \tan(\pi-\theta)
\end{align*}$$
Hence,
$$\begin{align*}
\theta+\frac{\pi}{6} &= \pi-\theta+n\pi \\
2\theta &= \frac{5\pi}{6}+n\pi \\
\theta &= \frac{5\pi}{12}+\frac{n\pi}{2}
\end{align*}$$
For $0\leq \theta\leq \pi$,
$$\begin{align*}
\theta &= \frac{5\pi}{12},\frac{11\pi}{12}
\end{align*}$$

$$\begin{align*}
y &= \frac{2}{5\cos(\theta-\alpha)}
\end{align*}$$
The turning points occur when $\cos(\theta-\alpha)=1$ or $\cos(\theta-\alpha)=-1$.
$$\begin{align*}
\theta &= \alpha, & y &= \frac{2}{5}, \\
\theta &= \alpha+\pi, & y &= -\frac{2}{5}
\end{align*}$$
Thus the turning points are $\left(\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{4}{3}\right),\frac{2}{5}\right)$ and $\left(\pi+\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{4}{3}\right),-\frac{2}{5}\right)$.

$$\begin{align*}
\cos 5x + \sin x &= 0 \\
\cos 5x &= -\sin x \\
\cos 5x &= \cos\left(x + \frac{\pi}{2}\right) \\
\end{align*}$$This yields two sets of general solutions: Case 1:$$\begin{align*}
5x &= 2k\pi + x + \frac{\pi}{2} \\
4x &= 2k\pi + \frac{\pi}{2} \\
x &= \frac{k\pi}{2} + \frac{\pi}{8}
\end{align*}$$For $0 \leq x \leq 2\pi$: $k=0 \implies x = \frac{\pi}{8}$ $k=1 \implies x = \frac{5\pi}{8}$ $k=2 \implies x = \frac{9\pi}{8}$ $k=3 \implies x = \frac{13\pi}{8}$ (4 solutions) Case 2:$$\begin{align*}
5x &= 2k\pi - \left(x + \frac{\pi}{2}\right) \\
6x &= 2k\pi - \frac{\pi}{2} \\
x &= \frac{k\pi}{3} - \frac{\pi}{12}
\end{align*}$$For $0 \leq x \leq 2\pi$: $k=1 \implies x = \frac{3\pi}{12} = \frac{\pi}{4}$ $k=2 \implies x = \frac{7\pi}{12}$ $k=3 \implies x = \frac{11\pi}{12}$ $k=4 \implies x = \frac{15\pi}{12} = \frac{5\pi}{4}$ $k=5 \implies x = \frac{19\pi}{12}$ $k=6 \implies x = \frac{23\pi}{12}$ (6 solutions) Total number of distinct solutions $= 4 + 6 = 10$. Correct answer: D.

